@model PairPageModel
@{
    var pair = $"{Model.BaseFamily}/{Model.QuoteFamily}";
    ViewData["Title"] = $"{pair} across venues — Arena — CryptoSmith X";
    ViewData["Eyebrow"] = $"Market monitor · {Model.BaseFamily} / {Model.QuoteFamily}";
    ViewData["CollectedFrom"] = Model.CollectedFrom;
    ViewData["CollectedTo"] = Model.CollectedTo;
    ViewData["RenderedAt"] = Model.RenderedAt;
    ViewData["ShowCollected"] = true;

    // Candle panels are drawn only where there is a line to draw. A venue with one bar or none gets
    // a sentence saying so, which is a fact about that venue; an empty axis would be a picture of
    // a market that was flat.
    var drawable = Model.Rows.Where(r => r.Candles.Present >= 2).ToList();
    var listings = Statement.Listings(Model.Rows);

    // The shared price scale is shared WITHIN A QUOTE ASSET, not across the page, and the reason is
    // the same one that scopes the verdicts and the bars (blueprint §6): the fold guarantees this
    // page can hold a book in USD beside a book in USDT, and one price axis across both would draw
    // two currencies as one number line. Inside a quote the shared scale is what the panels are for
    // — without it every panel autoscales to itself and a venue that moved two cents looks exactly
    // as volatile as one that moved forty.
    var scaleByQuote = drawable
        .GroupBy(r => r.Row.QuoteAsset, StringComparer.Ordinal)
        .ToDictionary(
            g => g.Key,
            g => (Lo: g.Min(r => r.Candles.Low!.Value), Hi: g.Max(r => r.Candles.High!.Value)),
            StringComparer.Ordinal);
}

<main class="a-main">

    <partial name="_Statement" model="Model" />

    @*
        The live button, and the sentence beside it.

        It sits OUTSIDE every [data-live-region] deliberately: a control that redrew itself on each
        update would lose its own state and its listener in the middle of the thing it started.

        It starts hidden and arena-live.js reveals it, the same argument as the INK button — with
        scripts off, or in a browser with no EventSource, this control could do nothing, and a
        control that does nothing is a small lie. The page below it is complete either way.

        The note is where a failure has to be said out loud. A stream that dies, a database signal
        that stops, a feed at capacity: every one of those looks, on a page whose subject is a
        market, exactly like a market that went quiet. So the note says which, in words, and the
        page falls back to what it does without a stream — ages that keep counting from instants
        that are still true.
    *@
    @*
        RouteUrl by NAME, for the reason the pair links on the list page give: the default route is
        registered first, so Url.Action resolves this to /Pairs/Live?baseFamily=… — the address the
        route constraints do not cover. The constraints are no longer the only thing standing
        between an anonymous caller and a held-open connection (PairAddress is), but a page that
        emits the unconstrained form of its own URL is a page teaching every reader the address we
        did not mean.
    *@
    @{
        var liveUrl = Url.RouteUrl("pair-live", new
        {
            controller = "Pairs",
            action = "Live",
            baseFamily = Model.BaseFamily,
            quoteFamily = Model.QuoteFamily
        });
    }
    <div class="a-liverow" data-live-url="@liveUrl">
        <button type="button" class="a-btn" id="a-live" aria-pressed="false"
                aria-describedby="a-live-what" hidden>Live</button>

        @*
            RELOAD, and it is not the browser's button said twice.

            It appears only once this document has gone stale under the reader, and it appears
            beside the sentence that says so, because the two ways out of a stale page are not
            equivalent and the reader has to be told which is which: reload asks the server for a
            fresh read of every figure, Live leaves this document standing and replaces the table as
            each collector pass lands. A reader looking at a page of `degraded` marks wants the
            first one. Hidden until arena-ages.js decides the page has aged, and hidden again the
            moment a live push replaces the fragments — at that point the document is new.
        *@
        <button type="button" class="a-btn" id="a-live-reload" hidden>Reload</button>

        @*
            WHAT THIS PAGE IS, in words, beside the control that changes it.

            The live button worked and nobody found it: 54 by 32 pixels, one word, and nothing to
            say what pressing it would do until it had been pressed. A control whose label is the
            name of a state the reader cannot see is not discoverable, it is guessable. So the
            sentence beside it names the state the page is IN — a snapshot whose ages count forward
            from the render — and names the button as the thing that changes it. Written by
            arena-ages.js and empty here on purpose: with scripts off the ages do not count forward,
            and a server-rendered sentence saying they do would be false on exactly the pages that
            cannot correct it.

            It is also where this page admits it has gone stale; see THE DOCUMENT'S OWN AGE in
            arena-ages.js for what that means and why it is stated once about the document rather
            than 500 times about the cells.

            role="status" AND NOT ONLY aria-describedby. The description is read when the Live button
            takes focus, which is a reader who has gone looking; this sentence also REWRITES ITSELF
            under a reader who has done nothing at all — a tab comes back from an hour in the
            background and a box appears saying the document is an hour old. Announced only on
            focus, the one fact that governs whether every figure on the page still means anything
            was the one fact a screen reader was not told, while the connection note beside it — the
            less consequential of the two — carried role="status" and was. It is polite, it is
            written once per change (arena-ages.js holds the last text and does not repaint it), and
            it is the only announcement the Reload button gets: that button appears silently, and
            the sentence that appears with it is the sentence that names it.
        *@
        <p class="a-live-what" id="a-live-what" role="status"></p>

        <span class="a-live-note" id="a-live-note" role="status"></span>
    </div>

    <partial name="_PairTable" model="Model" />

    <section style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--gap-block)">
        @*
            The candles bar, in the reference's order: name, what the panels are, the rule running
            off to the right, and the one control that belongs to this section. The rule used to sit
            between the name and the meta line, which left the right end of the bar empty and gave a
            right-aligned control nowhere to be.
        *@
        <div class="a-section-head">
            <span class="csx-eyebrow" style="color:var(--brand-deep)">Candles</span>
            <span class="csx-eyebrow" style="color:var(--text-faint)">
                Hourly · 24 h · price only · one scale per quote · time axes tied
            </span>
            <hr>

            @*
                HOW MANY PANELS SIT SIDE BY SIDE.

                Not decoration, and not a preference about density: the panels share one price scale
                per quote asset and one time axis across all of them, and a shared scale is only
                READABLE when the panels are stacked — two venues in one column put the same price at
                the same height on the page, which is the comparison the shared scale exists to make
                possible. Side by side, the reader is comparing two shapes at two different heights
                and has to do the arithmetic themselves. So the control offers the stack, and the
                glossary entry below says why anyone would want it.

                The buttons choose a CEILING, not a count. `.a-charts[data-cols]` keeps a readable
                minimum panel width in the same declaration, so a reader who asks for three on a
                laptop gets two — the alternative was a fixed `repeat(3, 1fr)` and 210-pixel candle
                panels, which is a picture of a chart rather than a chart.

                SO THE LABEL SAYS "UP TO". It read "3 columns" while the pressed button sat over a
                two-track grid on any window under about 1434px, and the choice is remembered, so
                that was sticky rather than momentary: press 3 on a desktop, open a pair on a laptop
                and the control names a layout the page is not in. On a page whose whole argument is
                that nothing claims a state it is not in, the control claimed one. Rejected: moving
                aria-pressed to whichever button matches the tracks actually drawn — the reader
                presses 3, the control lights 2, and a control that answers a press by refusing it
                is a worse lie than a loose label. One column has no ceiling to miss (the track is
                `minmax(0, 1fr)`, which always draws exactly one), so it is named exactly.

                Hidden until arena-candles.js reveals it, the same argument as the INK and LIVE
                buttons: with scripts off it could do nothing, and a control that does nothing is a
                small lie. The grid's scripts-off default packs as many panels as fit, which is what
                the page has always drawn, so nothing is lost by the control being absent.

                One panel is not an arrangement, so at one panel there is no control.
            *@
            @if (drawable.Count > 1)
            {
                <span class="a-cols" role="group" aria-label="Candle panel columns" hidden>
                    <span class="csx-eyebrow a-cols-label">Columns</span>
                    <span class="a-cols-set">
                        @* The icon is the layout it asks for: one <i> per column, each painted as
                           three stacked bars. Bars in markup would be nine empty elements per
                           button for a 12-pixel picture. *@
                        <button type="button" class="a-cols-btn" data-cols="3" title="Up to 3 columns" aria-pressed="false">
                            <span class="a-cols-icon" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
                        </button>
                        <button type="button" class="a-cols-btn" data-cols="2" title="Up to 2 columns" aria-pressed="false">
                            <span class="a-cols-icon" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i></span>
                        </button>
                        <button type="button" class="a-cols-btn" data-cols="1" title="1 column" aria-pressed="false">
                            <span class="a-cols-icon" aria-hidden="true"><i></i></span>
                        </button>
                    </span>
                </span>
            }
        </div>

        @if (drawable.Count == 0)
        {
            <div class="a-panel a-empty">
                <h2>No hourly bars</h2>
                <p class="a-note">
                    No venue on this page has two closed hourly bars in the last 24 hours. That is a
                    statement about the history we hold, not about whether the market traded.
                </p>
            </div>
        }
        else
        {
            <div class="a-charts">
                @foreach (var v in drawable)
                {
                    var r = v.Row;
                    var scale = scaleByQuote[r.QuoteAsset];
                    var series = v.Candles;

                    @* A window with no bar goes out as time only, with no OHLC. That reserves its
                       slot on the axis so the gap stays a gap; dropping it would slide the
                       surrounding candles together, and a venue that went dark for six hours would
                       draw as one that never stopped. *@
                    var json = System.Text.Json.JsonSerializer.Serialize(
                        series.Windows.Select((w, i) => new
                        {
                            time = new DateTimeOffset(DateTime.SpecifyKind(w, DateTimeKind.Utc)).ToUnixTimeSeconds(),
                            o = series.Bars[i]?.Open,
                            h = series.Bars[i]?.High,
                            l = series.Bars[i]?.Low,
                            c = series.Bars[i]?.Close
                        }));

                    var gaps = series.Windows.Count - series.Present;

                    <section class="a-chart-panel">
                        <header class="a-chart-head">
                            <h2 class="a-chart-title">
                                @r.ExchangeName
                                <span class="a-chart-symbol">@r.Symbol</span>
                            </h2>
                            <span class="a-chart-range">
                                @Format.Num(series.Low, Format.Decimals(r.PriceStep, fallback: 4)) –
                                @Format.Num(series.High, Format.Decimals(r.PriceStep, fallback: 4)) @r.QuoteAsset@(gaps > 0 ? $" · {gaps} of {series.Windows.Count} hours have no bar" : "")@(series.Partial > 0 ? $" · {series.Partial} partly covered" : "")
                            </span>
                        </header>
                        <div class="a-chart-body">
                            <div class="a-chart"
                                 data-candles="@json"
                                 data-scale-lo="@scale.Lo.ToString(System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)"
                                 data-scale-hi="@scale.Hi.ToString(System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)"
                                 data-decimals="@Format.Decimals(r.PriceStep, fallback: 4)"></div>
                        </div>
                    </section>
                }
            </div>

            @if (drawable.Count < listings)
            {
                @* LISTINGS, not venues. One panel is drawn per instrument, so a venue quoting the
                   pair against two quotes in the same family has two of them, and counting rows
                   under the word "venue" overstates how many order books this page could not draw
                   — the same substitution the statement line above used to make. *@
                <p class="a-note">
                    @(listings - drawable.Count) of @listings listing@(listings == 1 ? "" : "s")
                    @(listings - drawable.Count == 1 ? "has" : "have") fewer than two closed hourly bars in
                    this window and no panel is drawn for @(listings - drawable.Count == 1 ? "it" : "them").
                </p>
            }
        }
    </section>
    @* The paragraph that used to close this page — rows are not differenced, candles share their
       axes, hollow above the open — is not gone: it is two headed entries in the reading section
       below, THE ROWS and CANDLES. Two blocks of running prose stacked above the footer were the
       same wall the table's own note was, one section further down. *@
</main>

@*
    HOW TO READ THIS PAGE.

    A section, so the layout can put it in the footer: this is where the link under the table lands,
    and it has to be past the candles rather than in front of them. See _Layout.cshtml.

    It is one entry per rule, each under the name of the thing it explains, and that shape is the
    point of the whole change. This was a single ~350-word paragraph wedged between the table and
    the charts, which meant the reader who wanted one rule read thirteen and the reader who wanted
    the charts read thirteen they had not asked for. Every rule that paragraph stated is here, plus
    the ones the closing note held, and each of them can now be found by looking rather than by
    reading everything.

    Terms are markup, not data: they are the marks the table prints — △, CROSSED, BEST — and a
    reader matching what they saw against what it means is doing a lookup, so the words are set as
    labels and the entries stay in the order the eye meets them, from the age on a cell outwards.
*@
@section reading {
    <section class="a-reading" id="reading">
        @*
            NO RULE HERE, AND THE REFERENCE IS EXPLICIT ABOUT IT. Measured on the live mock: the
            candles head draws a 1px rule between its meta line and the columns control, and
            `#reading h2` is a bare heading with no sibling rule at all. That is not an oversight in
            the mock, it is the difference between the two pieces of furniture — the candles bar is
            a section head whose rule exists to carry the eye to a control at the right end, and
            this is a caption over a block of prose with nothing at its right end to reach. Reusing
            `.a-section-head` gave the footer a 1710px divider that reads as a section boundary
            where the reference reads as a label.

            The SIZE stays the design system's own eyebrow (9px / .17em) rather than the mock's
            hardcoded 10px / .18em. The mock is not token-aligned there — it writes the same two
            values inline on its candles head as well — and matching it would mean a raw font size
            in one view against a token used everywhere else on this surface.
        *@
        <h2 class="csx-eyebrow a-reading-title">How to read this page</h2>

        <div class="a-reading-grid">
            <div class="a-term">
                <span class="a-term-name">12 s ago</span>
                <span class="a-term-body">
                    The age of the call that wrote the figure, counted from the instant that call
                    landed. The figure is at full strength when it lands and fades across that
                    call's own window; each cell names its own window in its hover.
                </span>
            </div>

            <div class="a-term">
                @* The second sentence used to read "nothing is measured beyond it — a second over
                   and a week over read the same", which is true of the FADE (Freshness.Weight
                   clamps at one window) and false of everything the reader actually reads: the age
                   is still spelled out to the second, and the figure still takes its rank, until
                   the call goes degraded. The entry immediately below said the opposite, so the
                   glossary contradicted itself two terms apart. *@
                <span class="a-term-name">△</span>
                <span class="a-term-body">
                    That call is past its window, so the fade has run out — a second over and a week
                    over are drawn at the same strength. The count itself does not stop: the age is
                    still spelled out to the second and the figure still takes its rank, until the
                    call goes <b>degraded</b>.
                </span>
            </div>

            <div class="a-term">
                @* The multiple is interpolated from Freshness.DegradedWindows and never typed
                   here. A number in this sentence would be a fourth copy of a constant the server,
                   the client and the tests already share, and this repository still carries the
                   test file named after the last time one of those copies drifted. *@
                <span class="a-term-name">99+ s · degraded</span>
                <span class="a-term-body">
                    <b>99+ s</b> is the count still running, in a slot that does not widen to say how
                    far past a hundred it has gone. <b>degraded</b> replaces the count once the call
                    is @Freshness.DegradedWindows times its own window behind, where it has stopped
                    meaning anything; the feed is behind, not missing. A degraded figure takes no
                    rank and no bar: it sets no column maximum, wins nothing and loses nothing, and
                    when the call under a mark goes degraded while this page is open the column
                    drops both its marks and its bars rather than leave half a comparison standing.
                </span>
            </div>

            <div class="a-term">
                <span class="a-term-name">— and 0</span>
                <span class="a-term-body">
                    <b>—</b> is not measured and is never a zero. A <b>0</b> is an observation: a
                    band with nothing resting in it, which is a fact about that book. A listing that
                    carries no mark, index, funding or open interest shows dashes there.
                </span>
            </div>

            <div class="a-term">
                <span class="a-term-name">fresh / old</span>
                <span class="a-term-body">
                    The scale in the venue cell is measured in windows and not in seconds, so the
                    two names under it are the call least far into its own window and the call
                    furthest into its own — which on a venue whose depth pass is minutes wide is not
                    the same pair as the youngest and the oldest age. When any call on that row has
                    gone <b>degraded</b> both names are replaced by that fact, because a scale with a
                    dead end is not a scale.
                </span>
            </div>

            <div class="a-term">
                @* SCOPE IS PER COLUMN, AND THE ENTRY HAS TO SAY WHICH. The notebar sentence this
                   replaced carried the qualifier "for anything priced in the quote currency" and
                   was printed only on a page that actually held two quotes; the first rewrite
                   dropped both, and the unconditional claim it left behind is false for three of
                   the columns it names. Bid size, ask size and open interest are
                   VerdictScope.WholePair (Data/Verdicts.cs) — they are carried into base units by
                   contract_multiplier, so they are one measurement across every venue and are
                   ranked across every row on the page regardless of quote. A reader following the
                   old text read an acid BEST on open interest as "largest USDT book" when it means
                   "largest book on the page", which is the scope error this entry exists to
                   prevent, made by the entry. *@
                <span class="a-term-name">Best / worst</span>
                <span class="a-term-body">
                    The two ends of one column across the venues compared here: the highest bid, the
                    lowest ask, the largest turnover or depth — and the other end of each. Everything
                    between the ends carries no mark. Which rows are compared is decided per column,
                    not per page: anything priced in the quote currency ranks only against rows
                    quoting in the same asset, because a figure in USD and a figure in USDT are two
                    claims and a page holding two quotes holds two comparisons — while the two sizes
                    and open interest are in base units through the venue's own contract multiplier,
                    which makes them one measurement, so they rank across every row on the page.
                </span>
            </div>

            <div class="a-term">
                <span class="a-term-name">Tight / wide</span>
                <span class="a-term-body">
                    The spread column's own words for those two ends — the narrowest and the widest
                    spread among the rows compared, not a fixed number either side of which a spread
                    changes name. A spread in bps carries no currency, so this column compares every
                    row on the page whatever it quotes in. <b>Wide</b> takes the same chip as
                    <b>worst</b>, because it is the same verdict.
                </span>
            </div>

            <div class="a-term">
                <span class="a-term-name">Crossed</span>
                <span class="a-term-body">
                    That venue's bid stands at or above its ask. The figure beside the chip is the
                    negative spread that quotation implies, written as it stands, and the row is
                    left out of the spread ranking at both ends because a crossed book is not a
                    narrow one.
                </span>
            </div>

            <div class="a-term">
                <span class="a-term-name">Bar</span>
                <span class="a-term-body">
                    The figure against the largest venue in its own comparison, log-scaled — linear
                    flattens a sixty-unit book against a three-thousand-unit one to nothing, which
                    is the case these columns exist to show.
                </span>
            </div>

            <div class="a-term">
                <span class="a-term-name">Depth</span>
                <span class="a-term-body">
                    Bid / ask resting within that band of the mid. The mirrored bar under the pair
                    puts bid left of centre and ask right, so a one-sided book reads as a lopsided
                    bar rather than as a smaller one.
                </span>
            </div>

            <div class="a-term">
                <span class="a-term-name">Sizes · open interest</span>
                <span class="a-term-body">
                    In base units, through the venue's own contract multiplier, so a perp quoted in
                    thousand-token lots can be set beside a spot book. The venue's own figure is in
                    the hover.
                </span>
            </div>

            <div class="a-term">
                <span class="a-term-name">Funding</span>
                <span class="a-term-body">
                    Quoted per payment, carrying its interval and a normalised per-day figure. It is
                    <b>not ranked</b>: which direction is good depends on which side of the trade
                    you are on.
                </span>
            </div>

            <div class="a-term">
                <span class="a-term-name">The line under a figure</span>
                <span class="a-term-body">
                    That figure's last 25 hours, in its call's own colour, broken at every hour the
                    venue has no row for. Bid, ask and last share one line because they share one
                    price series.
                </span>
            </div>

            <div class="a-term">
                <span class="a-term-name">Candles</span>
                <span class="a-term-body">
                    Hourly, 24 h back, price only. One price scale per quote asset, and one time
                    axis across every panel — pan or zoom any one of them and the rest follow.
                    Hollow above the open, filled below: direction is not a result and takes no
                    money colour here. Stack them in one column with the control above them to
                    compare two venues by eye — the shared scale is what makes that reading valid,
                    and it is only legible when the same price sits at the same height.
                </span>
            </div>

            <div class="a-term">
                <span class="a-term-name">The rows</span>
                <span class="a-term-body">
                    Rows are not differenced against each other and this page states no cross-venue
                    spread from them: the poll passes are unsynchronised, a depth sweep across a
                    large venue takes minutes, and the interval is set per segment. The ages say
                    which.
                </span>
            </div>

            <div class="a-term">
                <span class="a-term-name">Ticker · oi · depth</span>
                <span class="a-term-body">
                    Three calls, three clocks. The bands above the columns say which call each
                    column came from, and a line under a figure takes that call's hue.
                </span>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>
}

@section scripts {
    @* Loaded only where a panel exists. A 198 KB charting library on a page with nothing to chart is
       198 KB spent to draw nothing. *@
    @if (drawable.Count > 0)
    {
        <script src="~/vendor/lightweight-charts/lightweight-charts-5.2.1.standalone.production.js?v=1"></script>
        <script src="~/arena-candles.js?v=2"></script>
    }

    @* 4 KB, on the pair page only, and it opens nothing until the button is pressed. *@
    <script src="~/arena-live.js?v=1" defer></script>
}
